From: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
Subject: [PATCH] moal: stop touching the receive descriptor after handing it up

woal_alloc_mlan_buffer() puts the mlan_buffer inside the skb it allocates:

	skb = __dev_alloc_skb(size + sizeof(mlan_buffer), flag);
	skb_reserve(skb, sizeof(mlan_buffer));
	pmbuf = (mlan_buffer *)skb->head;

So the descriptor and the packet share one allocation, and freeing the skb
frees the descriptor with it. moal_recv_packet() does not observe that.
After it hands the skb to netif_rx() or netif_receive_skb() -- which
consume it, and on this path complete inline, so the frame is routed,
answered and freed before the call returns -- it goes on to read pmbuf
twice more:

	done:
		if (status != MLAN_STATUS_PENDING && pmbuf && !pmbuf->pdesc && skb)
			dev_kfree_skb(skb);
		if (pmbuf && !pmbuf->pbuf)
			status = MLAN_STATUS_PENDING;

Both dereference memory the stack has already returned to the allocator,
and the first can free the skb a second time as well: MLAN_STATUS_PENDING
is how this function says "someone else owns this now", and the delivery
paths never set it -- only the branch that receives an skb already
attached to the buffer does.

An access point triggers this within seconds of a client associating:
every ARP reply and every IPv6 multicast frame is delivered, consumed and
freed inline. KASAN on an LS1046A, once per frame:

  BUG: KASAN: slab-use-after-free in moal_recv_packet+0x3890/0x4d44 [moal]
  Read of size 8 at addr ffff00084fac4028 by task kworker/u17:3/152
  Workqueue: MOAL_PCIE_RX_WORK_QUEUE woal_pcie_rx_work_queue [moal]
   moal_recv_packet+0x3890/0x4d44 [moal]
   wlan_process_uap_rx_packet+0x5cc/0x124c [mlan]
   woal_pcie_rx_work_queue+0x180/0x1fc [moal]
  Freed by task 152:
   __kfree_skb <- consume_skb <- arp_process <- arp_rcv

Same task both times. Without KASAN it is silent corruption of whatever
reuses the slab, and what it presents as is an access point that faults
mid-handshake -- which the client reports as an authentication failure.

Fix both halves. Say that the skb has been handed off, once, after the
delivery chain, so the second free cannot happen; and do not consult the
descriptor at all once it has, since it no longer exists. The remaining
work under `done:` is only meaningful for a buffer that was *not* handed
up, so gating the whole epilogue on that is both correct and narrower than
the reads it removes.

Upstream-Status: Pending [reported to NXP; moal_recv_packet() dereferences
the mlan_buffer after the skb it is embedded in has been given to the
network stack, and frees that skb a second time]

Signed-off-by: Tomaz Zaman <tomaz@mono.si>
---
 mlinux/moal_shim.c | 35 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++---------
 1 file changed, 26 insertions(+), 9 deletions(-)

--- a/mlinux/moal_shim.c
+++ b/mlinux/moal_shim.c
@@ -3111,6 +3111,15 @@
 					woal_get_monotonic_time(&t);
 					pmbuf->out_ts_sec = t.time_sec;
 					pmbuf->out_ts_usec = t.time_usec;
+					/* Accounted here rather than after the
+					 * handoff below. woal_alloc_mlan_buffer()
+					 * places the mlan_buffer at skb->head, so
+					 * once the stack has the skb this
+					 * descriptor is freed memory; a few
+					 * microseconds early is the only legal
+					 * time to read it. */
+					moal_tp_accounting(handle, pmbuf,
+							   RX_TIME_PKT);
 				}
 			}
 			if (priv->phandle->tp_acnt.drop_point == RX_DROP_P4) {
@@ -3136,18 +3145,27 @@
 					}
 				}
 			}
-			if (priv->phandle->tp_acnt.on) {
-				if (pmbuf && pmbuf->in_ts_sec)
-					moal_tp_accounting(handle, pmbuf,
-							   RX_TIME_PKT);
-			}
+			/* Every arm above consumes the skb, and the
+			 * mlan_buffer lives at skb->head, so pmbuf is freed
+			 * with it. Record the handoff before anything can read
+			 * either again; the RX_TIME_PKT accounting that used
+			 * to sit here now runs just above, while pmbuf is
+			 * still live.
+			 */
+			status = MLAN_STATUS_PENDING;
 		}
 	}
 done:
-	if (status != MLAN_STATUS_PENDING && pmbuf && !pmbuf->pdesc && skb)
-		dev_kfree_skb(skb);
-	if (pmbuf && !pmbuf->pbuf)
-		status = MLAN_STATUS_PENDING;
+	/* Nothing below may touch pmbuf once the skb carrying it is gone, and
+	 * MLAN_STATUS_PENDING is precisely the record of that. Both statements
+	 * only ever meant anything for a buffer that was never handed up.
+	 */
+	if (status != MLAN_STATUS_PENDING) {
+		if (pmbuf && !pmbuf->pdesc && skb)
+			dev_kfree_skb(skb);
+		if (pmbuf && !pmbuf->pbuf)
+			status = MLAN_STATUS_PENDING;
+	}
 	LEAVE();
 	return status;
 }
